<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The macOS app's entitlements, for local development.

  NO APP SANDBOX HERE, DELIBERATELY

  The sandbox is not the problem — the App Group is. It is provisioning-profile-backed, so
  an ad-hoc-signed build cannot have one, and App Groups cannot be registered on a free
  Apple ID at all. Without a group, a sandboxed app writes into its own container and the
  AUv3 extension cannot read a byte of it: an empty project list in Logic, with nothing
  anywhere to say why.

  So the ad-hoc arrangement puts the app's write path outside a container. The app writes
  to `~/Library/Application Support/Unplugged` (see src/shared_container.rs) and the
  extension — which stays sandboxed — reads it through the read-only temporary exception in
  plugin/Support/UnpluggedAU.entitlements.

  `device.audio-input` stays regardless: under the hardened runtime it is what lets a
  signed build reach the microphone. The usage description in Info.plist governs the
  prompt, but without this the prompt is never reached.

  The shipping arrangement — sandbox and App Group on the app, the extension and the host
  stub alike — is in Entitlements-Signed.plist, and needs a paid Apple Developer
  membership:

      npm run tauri build -- --config src-tauri/tauri.signed.conf.json
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
